[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 3.Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 4.d5 5.e4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/43d1eef6-783d-4a92-82f4-cffa73e21354/chapters/1bc05e9e-030a-4fc7-9503-4a102bc55e5e/studies/0f87aaef-c008-4b57-9368-982bef7b893d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Queen's Gambit Accepted! This is a popular opening. The odd thing about this gambit is that people do not always expect the capture of the c4-pawn and declining is more common. This gives our approach the element of surprise. White starts with 1.d4, and we'll reply with ...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit. There are more than 200 million games in the database of it. We will accept the gambit. One of our goals is simply to develop our pieces and let White waste time recapturing the c4-pawn.} 2...dxc4 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is the most popular move under 2000 rating. There are more than 18 million games in the database. It isn't particularly good because it doesn't prevent our 3...e5 move and it doesn't get closer to taking on c4. We'll go ahead and reply with ...e5!} 3...e5 4.d5 {This popular advance can also be reached via 3.e4. 4.d5 is played nearly 47% of times and has more than 850,000 games in the database. We will develop and start putting pressure on the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.e4 {Close to 90% of games lead us to this position, which is our main line. We should inconvenience White by defending c4. They now have to decide whether to take on b5 or not. If they don't take, we defend c4. If they do take, they lose e4!} 5...b5 6.Bg5 {White could also pin us. Now we should develop our dark-squared bishop. c5 is an aggressive location, with an hidden idea.} 6...Bc5 7.Nxb5 {This capture falls into our trap. Now we can capture on f2 with check and set up a fork.} 7...Bxf2+ 8.Kxf2 {White takes. We'll take on e4 with check.} 8...Nxe4+ 9.Ke3 {This is a very bad move. We'll take the bishop with check.} 9...Qxg5+ 10.Kxe4 {If they take the knight...} 10...Qf4# {That's checkmate!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 3.Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 4.d5 5.e4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/43d1eef6-783d-4a92-82f4-cffa73e21354/chapters/1bc05e9e-030a-4fc7-9503-4a102bc55e5e/studies/0f87aaef-c008-4b57-9368-982bef7b893d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Queen's Gambit Accepted! This is a popular opening. The odd thing about this gambit is that people do not always expect the capture of the c4-pawn and declining is more common. This gives our approach the element of surprise. White starts with 1.d4, and we'll reply with ...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit. There are more than 200 million games in the database of it. We will accept the gambit. One of our goals is simply to develop our pieces and let White waste time recapturing the c4-pawn.} 2...dxc4 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is the most popular move under 2000 rating. There are more than 18 million games in the database. It isn't particularly good because it doesn't prevent our 3...e5 move and it doesn't get closer to taking on c4. We'll go ahead and reply with ...e5!} 3...e5 4.d5 {This popular advance can also be reached via 3.e4. 4.d5 is played nearly 47% of times and has more than 850,000 games in the database. We will develop and start putting pressure on the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.e4 {Close to 90% of games lead us to this position, which is our main line. We should inconvenience White by defending c4. They now have to decide whether to take on b5 or not. If they don't take, we defend c4. If they do take, they lose e4!} 5...b5 6.Nxb5 {The most popular move is 6.Nxb5. White simply captures. I suspect many people don't even see they're losing e4. This is good for us. Our active knight will combine with our bishop which is coming to b4 with check.} 6...Nxe4 7.Bxc4 {White captures on c4. They also develop the bishop. However, our edge is clear. Now Bb4+ is awkward for White to meet.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Kf1 {White loses castling rights. In contrast, we'll castle and get our king into safety.} 8...O-O 9.Nf3 {White develops their knight. We'll attack the knight and the pawn. The d-pawn is also pinned.} 9...c6 10.Nc3 {If the knight goes back, we can at the very minimum win a pawn. But the position is worse than that for White. We'll take on c3 with the bishop.} 10...Bxc3 11.bxc3 {They recapture. And we'll take on c3 with the knight, hitting both the queen and the d5-pawn.} 11...Nxc3 12.Qb3 {Now Qb3 attacks our knight. But we don't even have to move it away. We can take another pawn and attack the bishop by playing cxd5.} 12...cxd5 13.Qxc3 {The queen takes our knight.} 13...dxc4 {And we take the bishop. White cannot even take on c4 because of ...Ba6. White is totally lost.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 3.Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 4.d5 5.e4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/43d1eef6-783d-4a92-82f4-cffa73e21354/chapters/1bc05e9e-030a-4fc7-9503-4a102bc55e5e/studies/0f87aaef-c008-4b57-9368-982bef7b893d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Queen's Gambit Accepted! This is a popular opening. The odd thing about this gambit is that people do not always expect the capture of the c4-pawn and declining is more common. This gives our approach the element of surprise. White starts with 1.d4, and we'll reply with ...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit. There are more than 200 million games in the database of it. We will accept the gambit. One of our goals is simply to develop our pieces and let White waste time recapturing the c4-pawn.} 2...dxc4 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is the most popular move under 2000 rating. There are more than 18 million games in the database. It isn't particularly good because it doesn't prevent our 3...e5 move and it doesn't get closer to taking on c4. We'll go ahead and reply with ...e5!} 3...e5 4.d5 {This popular advance can also be reached via 3.e4. 4.d5 is played nearly 47% of times and has more than 850,000 games in the database. We will develop and start putting pressure on the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.e4 {Close to 90% of games lead us to this position, which is our main line. We should inconvenience White by defending c4. They now have to decide whether to take on b5 or not. If they don't take, we defend c4. If they do take, they lose e4!} 5...b5 6.Nxb5 {The most popular move is 6.Nxb5. White simply captures. I suspect many people don't even see they're losing e4. This is good for us. Our active knight will combine with our bishop which is coming to b4 with check.} 6...Nxe4 7.Bxc4 {White captures on c4. They also develop the bishop. However, our edge is clear. Now Bb4+ is awkward for White to meet.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Kf1 {White loses castling rights. In contrast, we'll castle and get our king into safety.} 8...O-O 9.Nf3 {White develops their knight. We'll attack the knight and the pawn. The d-pawn is also pinned.} 9...c6 10.Na3 {White is forced to the bad a3-square.} 10...Bb7 {We can simply develop and attack the pawn. This position is -2. We are clearly winning. Qb3 does nothing because of ...a5.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: White Plays 3.Nc3 / Study 1: White Plays 4.d5 5.e4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/43d1eef6-783d-4a92-82f4-cffa73e21354/chapters/1bc05e9e-030a-4fc7-9503-4a102bc55e5e/studies/0f87aaef-c008-4b57-9368-982bef7b893d/lines"]

1.d4 {Welcome to the Queen's Gambit Accepted! This is a popular opening. The odd thing about this gambit is that people do not always expect the capture of the c4-pawn and declining is more common. This gives our approach the element of surprise. White starts with 1.d4, and we'll reply with ...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit. There are more than 200 million games in the database of it. We will accept the gambit. One of our goals is simply to develop our pieces and let White waste time recapturing the c4-pawn.} 2...dxc4 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is the most popular move under 2000 rating. There are more than 18 million games in the database. It isn't particularly good because it doesn't prevent our 3...e5 move and it doesn't get closer to taking on c4. We'll go ahead and reply with ...e5!} 3...e5 4.d5 {This popular advance can also be reached via 3.e4. 4.d5 is played nearly 47% of times and has more than 850,000 games in the database. We will develop and start putting pressure on the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.e4 {Close to 90% of games lead us to this position, which is our main line. We should inconvenience White by defending c4. They now have to decide whether to take on b5 or not. If they don't take, we defend c4. If they do take, they lose e4!} 5...b5 6.Nxb5 {The most popular move is 6.Nxb5. White simply captures. I suspect many people don't even see they're losing e4. This is good for us. Our active knight will combine with our bishop which is coming to b4 with check.} 6...Nxe4 7.Bxc4 {White captures on c4. They also develop the bishop. However, our edge is clear. Now Bb4+ is awkward for White to meet.} 7...Bb4+ 8.Kf1 {White loses castling rights. In contrast, we'll castle and get our king into safety.} 8...O-O 9.a3 {White can kick our bishop away.} 9...Bc5 {But it's very obvious that White's opening hasn't gone well. We have the edge: their king is on f1. We will play ...c6 and blast the position open.} *

