1.d4 {Welcome to the Queen's Gambit Accepted! This is a popular opening. The odd thing about this gambit is that people do not always expect the capture of the c4-pawn and declining is more common. This gives our approach the element of surprise. White starts with 1.d4, and we'll reply with ...d5.} 1...d5 2.c4 {This is the Queen's Gambit. There are more than 200 million games in the database of it. We will accept the gambit. One of our goals is simply to develop our pieces and let White waste time recapturing the c4-pawn.} 2...dxc4 3.Nc3 {3.Nc3 is the most popular move under 2000 rating. There are more than 18 million games in the database. It isn't particularly good because it doesn't prevent our 3...e5 move and it doesn't get closer to taking on c4. We'll go ahead and reply with ...e5!} 3...e5 4.d5 {This popular advance can also be reached via 3.e4. 4.d5 is played nearly 47% of times and has more than 850,000 games in the database. We will develop and start putting pressure on the pawn.} 4...Nf6 5.e4 {Close to 90% of games lead us to this position, which is our main line. We should inconvenience White by defending c4. They now have to decide whether to take on b5 or not. If they don't take, we defend c4. If they do take, they lose e4!} 5...b5 6.Bg5 {White could also pin us. Now we should develop our dark-squared bishop. c5 is an aggressive location, with an hidden idea.} 6...Bc5 7.Nxb5 {This capture falls into our trap. Now we can capture on f2 with check and set up a fork.} 7...Bxf2+ 8.Kxf2 {White takes. We'll take on e4 with check.} 8...Nxe4+ 9.Ke1 {The king goes to e1.} 9...Nxg5 {We take with our knight because we need the queen to keep an eye on c7.} *

